Z||000000||Richard Parnell||11-07-2000||06:19 PM||||We got the fuel pump to working but now we found that when we start up the engine that the clutch does not engage.  We have access to another trans if we need to change it out.  But before we go to the hassle of all that we need to see if someone has had any experience with this V8 archie kit similar to what we are having.  The trans that is in the car is the same one that was being used with the 4 cyl.<BR>The transmission shifts.<BR>When we start the car up and let out the clutch there is no engagement with the pressure plate.<BR>With the other engine the clutch was shot so it could not disengage but I could still double clutch and shift with some grinding.<BR>The question we have is that we noticed that the adapter plate we got with the V8 archie kit seemed pretty thick.<BR>We are wondering if the plate is too thick to allow the clutch to reach the pressure plate. We are attempting to get in contact with Archie right now but in the meantime I'm throwing out this request for information from all the Fiero people out there. Help.<BR>I don't want to change out the trans and still have this problem.||||reg||8
Z||000001||StuGood||11-08-2000||01:06 PM||||No experience with Archie kit, but...<P>Stupid suggestion: You <B>did</B> put in the (new) clutch disk, I hope  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?  Stu's done stupider things  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ...    <P>Could try (temporarily) un-mounting the slave cylinder, and let the clutch fork rotate freely (engine off, of course).  If you get engagement now, it points to T/O bearing-to-release fingers geometry problems.   I <B>think</B> the fork should back off freely at some point - in other words, if you rotate the fork enough, it should come off the clutch release fingers completely (you'd feel it).         <P>How thick is the adapter plate?  Is there supposed to be some kind of spacer under the pressure plate to match ?  ||||reg||5
